00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.390 align:start position:0%
 
good afternoon everyone  thank you for joining us for the media  availability with ottawa police interim  chief steve bell  joining us are deputy commissioner  chris harkins with the ontario  provincial police  rcmp deputy commissioner mike gm  federal policing  and city of ottawa  general manager emergency and protective  services kim ayotte  press  chef  steve bell  kim ayotte  i'm corporal vladimir napoleon with the  rcmp  i will be the moderator  we will begin with remarks from  internship  interim chief bell  followed by general manager kim ayotte  and then we will proceed to a 15-minute  question period  [Music]  today we're  today we've made some very important  progress in safely removing this  unlawful protest from our streets  at every step in this operation we've  been up front and clear with the  unlawful protesters that they must leave  the area  we've advised them of our enforcement's  efforts so they could continue to make  informed decisions  we've backed up those warnings with a  deliberate and methodical operation  using lawful and safe tactics  the officers on the ground have shown  patience and professionalism  they are well trained and experienced  and they needed all of that expertise to  bring about the results that we are  seeing at every step they faced a  barrage of resistance shoving and  vitriol  we are we all saw that this is the  protesters were aggressive with the  officers and we needed to use horses at  one point  as a result we responded this morning by  adding helmets and batons to our  equipment for the safety of our officers  when we deployed our officers this  morning you saw the public order units  on the ground sweeping across the  downtown core from east to west and  moving demonstrators out of the  parliamentary precinct  our officers continued to face  resistance  at one point a flare was ignited by a  protester officers used a chemical  irritant also known as pepper spray to  disperse unlawful demonstrators who were  resisting police orders  sadly we again saw young children being  brought to the unlawful demonstration  and to the front of our police operation  this is dangerous and is putting young  children at risk  so far today we've arrested 47 people  that brings the total so far to 170.  as we continue to take back our streets  our streets city of ottawa crews and  other municipal vehicles came into the  area to clean up and tow away all of the  demonstrators possessions and vehicles  this is tough work  these officers are doing what they've  been trained to do they have  demonstrated unparalleled discipline  restraint and the utmost professionalism  i commend them for their actions  yesterday and throughout the day today  i'm proud of them and all the members of  our police service that have contributed  to this operation  justification  service and natural community pandon set  period difficile  this is an ottawa-led police joint  operation but this is actually a  canadian effort  we have officers from across the  province and country here to help us  every person supporting this effort is  proud to be here  police have a vital role in our  democracy and this operation shows our  institutions commitment and ability to  maintain a lawful society  but we could do not do this without the  support of the community that we serve  all levels of government the federal  provincial and municipal have  contributed to this effort all to  support our city and every other  community being affected by similar  unlawful demonstrations  by working together we are showing the  strength of our democracy  to downtown residents we are focused on  your safety  we are not going anywhere until you have  your streets back  we are prepared we are aware of  protesters leaving the parliamentary  precinct moving to surrounding  neighborhoods we are working with our  partners and we will remove these  protesters peacefully from your area  this operation is still moving forward  it is not over and it will takes takes  up more time until we have achieved our  goals  uh following uh mission comments i'll be  available to answer questions  thank you chief bill  good afternoon everyone  the city continues to support our police  service who made excellent progress  towards ending this occupation  i want to thank the police leadership  and every single police officer from the  many different forces who are involved  in this operation  you are doing great work  i also want to thank and recognize city  staff who've been working day and night  over the past few weeks under  challenging conditions  whether you're working in public safety  emergency management in the eoc or in  the ncrcc whether you're public works  social services media relations  or working for or in a city facility  whether your corporate security or  behind the scenes in a support capacity  i want to thank you  i specifically want to thank our bylaw  officers who've issued over 3 600  tickets  and our fire services firefighters and  paramedics who are working in the red  zone as part of an integrated an  integration team directly with police  special teams  or responding to regular calls within  the red zone  to the city leadership team thank you  for your constant support and  availability during these difficult  times  you have been strong during this very  difficult and unprecedented event  please know that the city is always  supporting activities is already  supporting activities aimed at restoring  the downtown neighborhoods to their  normal state  this includes removing debris garbage  vehicles clearing snow installing fences  and repairing any damage left behind by  these protesters  thank you and merci  i will now invite each media agency to  come to the microphone to ask a question  please ask one question each  starting with your starting with your  name and the media that you represent  [Music]  represented  ctv national news  thanks for taking our questions  can you say when will residents have  their streets back do you have a day in  mind as wellington is now cleared out  so we've been  we've been clear from the beginning this  is this is an extremely large complex  operation  you all understand that because you've  been so actively covering and reporting  on on it since it's begun  we we won't commit to any end time  because the the progress of it needs to  be measured needs to be methodical needs  to be safe and lawful as we've indicated  all the way through that is how we've  approached this protest since we've  started that is how we will approach it  until it's completed  i cannot overstate we are in this until  it is over every one of our police  agency partners has indicated their  continued and ongoing support to work  with us to finish this unlawful  occupation you have our commitment that  we are here until the very end if i had  a choice it would be right now  and that's why i implore all the  protesters who remain there go home this  occupation is now over we will continue  with our mission until it is complete  and can you explain the chemical  irritant that was used against the  protesters by police today what kind of  chemical uh was it and and why was it  used and and you also uh  my other question here that i really  need that that's important that i want  to squeeze in here is  you talk about the professionalism of  police but with respect sir can you  explain what appears to be excessive  force by police officers when a  protester is arrested there is multiple  videos on social media showing police  delivering blows with their knee and  their fists while the protesters are  down is this necessary  so i i haven't specifically seen the  videos you're talking about but what i  cannot advise you is we do have  mechanisms to review all of the arrests  we will absolutely be involved in  reviewing arrests to make sure that the  lawful amount of force was used as we  executed our mission  we've been very clear from the beginning  as this has escalated and as protesters  have not heeded the the urging the  warnings and then the direction to leave  our streets that we would need to  escalate in our tactics that is what  you're seeing us engaged in to  ultimately remove people who are  refusing to leave  so we see that you guys with the help of  your other agencies have practically  gained all of wellington where  parliament hill is located so can you  tell us what's the next step into the  operation and are are the forces  starting to stretch thin as as you gain  terrain you're going to need more people  so what's the next step in the operation  and maybe how we're doing with officers  so what you've seen and we indicated  from the very beginning days this has  been going to be a very deliberate and  methodical approach to how we're going  to remove how we're going to gain back  the area and remove everyone to execute  the plan we have we also indicated that  we have the resources we need to execute  this plan what you've seen over the past  number of days is the execution of that  plan with the resources that we need to  finalize it we are approaching areas our  members are taking control of areas and  then we're bringing crews behind us to  remove vehicles remediate any of the  conditions that the protesters any of  the damage the protesters may have done  to make sure that as our citizens get  their streets back they are the streets  that they had before this started we  will continue with our plan we'll  continue to move ahead and i can tell  you it is to this point going exactly as  we had anticipated  um  have the police say  fire harm since the beginning of this  major policy intervention  so what i can tell you is that we have  several criminal investigations ongoing  that relate to the seizure of weapons i  don't have specific  specifics on any of those investigations  because they're not yet complete and  there is no charges resulting from them  when we do have that we will provide  that information  hi ashley burke cbc national news um can  you tell us what other methods that you  use today to try and disperse crowds  that we didn't see yesterday it sounded  like there was other sort of sonic or  you know other sort of sound things that  were done today that hadn't been the  past and what else  yeah we've been pretty clear from the  beginning we're actually not going to  discuss the tactics we use  one of the things that you've seen that  we've been engaged in is we we were  monitoring we've learned from previous  protests and demonstrations we're  monitoring the coverage  one of the tactics that the protesters  and demonstrators are using is  misinformation uh information that is  inaccurate uh information that misleads  the public  we're actively tweeting and providing  information out in through social media  and other media  mechanisms in order to be able to try  and get accurate factual information  around what is going on within the  protest without actually  divulging any police tactics  and just speaking of conflicting reports  yesterday there were a lot of them when  it came to the the case where the police  horse entered the crowd um ottawa police  had said that a bicycle was thrown at  the feet of a horse  but there are conflicting reports online  about that can you specifically tell us  what happened and what happened to the  civilian and the horse  so thank you um that when you talk about  him misinformation that's specifically  one of the one of the demonstrations for  us of that um  what what happened is the the crowd was  escalating around the police there was  tension that was continuing to escalate  between our public order unit members  and the demonstrators on the line that  was  resulting in increased aggression by the  demonstrators  members of the public over mounted  public order unit rode through the crowd  that created separation and lowered the  temperature and um and circumstances  that existed there so as not to escalate  force  almost immediately there was  tweets there was pictures that had been  photoshopped out  on social media that indicated uh people  had died as a result of that we the  horses had run over  somebody with a walker in the crowd what  i can indicate is there was two members  of the protesters who did collide with  the horses they fell down they  immediately got back up and started to  again engage in in their protest and  demonstration activity that is one of  the circumstances where we as we monitor  we're identifying this information and  as quickly as we can providing accurate  real information about what is occurring  on the ground  sorry but was a bike actually thrown at  a horse because there are images online  that might maybe perhaps it was someone  else's uh electronic wheelchair  so we'll we'll have to get more specific  information on that for you and i'll be  able to provide more in coming days  um hi chief phil it's juan canelo from  cbc ottawa local um you say you've  arrested about 170 people since the  police action has begun how many folks  have you actually charged  so we're continuing to uh  one step is the arrest the next step is  the pro processing and charging of those  individuals we haven't yet  pulled together and identified the  number of charges that we've laid but  that will be  released to the media and to the public  as soon as it's available but are people  being let go for example without being  charged with anything say if they  promise to go home  so what i can indicate again is we've  arrested 170 people we'll continue to do  the investigations and as we've  identified those that are charged and  those that may be released  unconditionally those numbers will  provide them to the media and as this is  a massive operation any chance there's a  price tag on this yet i think earlier on  when we had asked for 1800 officers  there was an indication it might cost  2.5 million dollars a day  is that accurate or i know it's probably  not exact at this moment so it's uh it's  something that's very important to us we  know that this is going to be an  extremely expensive operation uh we've  already talked to our federal and  provincial partners about  how at the end of the day we we will  look at  having money provided to the police  service for that those uh discussions  are ongoing we continue to track our  costs we our partners continue to work  with us to identify the costs of  policing to actually manage this event  and at the culmination of it we'll be  able to provide accurate figures you  don't have a tally up until now for  example just to remind our journalists  one question thank you  hi chief bill gabriel i'm from the  french radio of cbc just wondering do  you know how many buildings are in  lockdown  right now in ottawa and  i will take a response in french also  please  so we we don't identify buildings to go  into lockdown we don't tell building  owners to put their buildings in  lockdown we do provide public safety  announcements as to where  the  the  demonstration is happening where our  police operations are occurring within  the demonstration so we don't directly  control the number of buildings which  are locked down so i don't have that  number for you  sorry  hi there uh erika ibrahim the canadian  press um  i'm wondering uh what your message is to  protesters and canadians more broadly  who might be upset and angry with uh  images uh being published of police  hitting protesters with batons and other  types of force  there are images of some people um there  are some police striking crowd members  with batons  who were retreating and not being  aggressive toward them there were media  in the crowds that that have witnessed  and reported on this i'm wondering what  you would say to um to those who may be  concerned by that  as i indicated earlier we will have the  opportunity to review all of those files  there are complaint mechanisms for  people to enter into if they feel that  there is excessive use of force  by members of our police service  what i will also say is  we have been here for three weeks i have  been at this podium for the last five  days imploring people to leave  asking them to get out of our streets  this occupation is over we have advised  them that if they peacefully leave they  may go home that still exists  we also indicated that we would escalate  and forcefully remove people from the  streets if they did not comply some of  that is what you're seeing so i will  stand here today again and say this  demonstration is over go home if you  don't go home we will remove you from  the streets  this will be the last question  hello i'm andy liam uh from independent  media so i was in the crowd yesterday um  i was unfortunately hit with some pepper  spray i just have a question uh  there's some video cameras that the  police are using and some news outlets  that reporting that you're gathering  intelligence with those cameras  can you elaborate like if the protesters  at this point  uh you know uh retreat and go home  uh are they gonna be getting sort of  repercussions down the road are you  going to be sort of actively pursuing  the people that you've been sort of  documenting and filming who are still  out there protesting what are your plans  after this after the protest is over  thank you it's a great question and the  simple answer is yes  if you are involved in this protest we  will actively look to identify you and  follow up with financial sanctions and  criminal charges absolutely  this investigation will go on for months  to come it has many many different  streams both from a federal  financial level from a provincial  licensing level from a criminal code  level from a municipal breach of court  order breach of court and junction level  it will be a complicated and  time-consuming  investigation that will go on for a  period of time  you have my commitment that that  investigation will continue and we will  hold people accountable for taking our  streets over  and could i just ask one more question  this concludes the  media availability  [ __ ] concluded point please  um  [Music]  you

